    {
      "id": "c5d7ddb16f0c3659aa929da378b3c0bc605a860d",
      "individual_note": false,
      "resolvable": true,
      "resolved": false,
      "notes": [
        {
          "id": 1921941237,
          "type": "DiscussionNote",
          "body": "@johnthacker Please have a look at this.\n\nWe *could* make this into a setting if desired, but I'm not convinced that's needed.\n\nIf we do make this into a setting we can do this in different ways:\n\n1. Let people set which flags they want to assume\n\nexample:\n[x] DEPRECATE_EOF\n[ ] Query Attributes\n\n2. Let people pick a MySQL/MariaDB version they want to assume\n\nexample:\nAssume Server Implementation: MySQL 8.4 / MySQL 8.0 / MySQL 5.7 / MariaDB 11.3 / MariaDB 11.2\n\n3. Let people copy/set a hex value\n\nServer Capabilities:          0xf7fe\nServer Capabilities Extended: 0x81bf\n\n----------------\n\nWe have caps and caps_ext. Should users be concerned about this for this setting? Or should it be a combined list?\n\nFor query attributes the server capabilities (`MYSQL_CAPS_QA`) isn't enough as this is only used if the client is also setting this. Should there be different settings for client and server capabilities?",
          "author": {
            "id": 5884947,
            "username": "dveeden",
            "public_email": "",
            "name": "Daniël van Eeden",
            "state": "active",
            "locked": false,
            "avatar_url": "https://secure.gravatar.com/avatar/00c536bb5304097eb409787c4a770e820db2a200d04130b8ca03cbcda0f526f7?s=80&d=identicon",
            "web_url": "https://gitlab.com/dveeden"
          },
          "created_at": "2024-05-25T14:22:51.149Z",
          "updated_at": "2024-05-27T05:52:28.803Z",
          "system": false,
          "noteable_id": 304252480,
          "noteable_type": "MergeRequest",
          "project_id": 7898047,
          "resolvable": true,
          "resolved": false,
          "resolved_by": null,
          "resolved_at": null,
          "confidential": false,
          "internal": false,
          "imported": false,
          "imported_from": "none",
          "noteable_iid": 15741,
          "commands_changes": {}
        },
        {
          "id": 1922545971,
          "type": "DiscussionNote",
          "body": "the better will be to have option 1 i think but may be make a lot of preference settings ?\n\noption 3 is good idea to (and my first idea if i need to implement this...)",
          "author": {
            "id": 279822,
            "username": "alagoutte",
            "public_email": "",
            "name": "Alexis La Goutte",
            "state": "active",
            "locked": false,
            "avatar_url": "https://secure.gravatar.com/avatar/a15cba6417f4d1723eb56add7099bcfd8df373dc520577958b33c37915ef231b?s=80&d=identicon",
            "web_url": "https://gitlab.com/alagoutte"
          },
          "created_at": "2024-05-27T05:52:28.719Z",
          "updated_at": "2024-05-27T05:52:28.719Z",
          "system": false,
          "noteable_id": 304252480,
          "noteable_type": "MergeRequest",
          "project_id": 7898047,
          "resolvable": true,
          "resolved": false,
          "resolved_by": null,
          "resolved_at": null,
          "confidential": false,
          "internal": false,
          "imported": false,
          "imported_from": "none",
          "noteable_iid": 15741,
          "commands_changes": {}
        },
        {
          "id": 1922581908,
          "type": "DiscussionNote",
          "body": "The issues I see with option 1 are:\n\nA. People might not know what versions set what flags. So they might not know that they need to set the `DEPRECATE_EOF` and/or query attributes flags if result sets fail to decode properly.\n\nB. People might set weird/unexpected combinations. Not sure how much of a problem this really is. Not sure if there is a good way to validate this. An example might be if someone sets `DEPRECATE_EOF` without setting `PROTOCOL_41`.\n\nC. Naming. We have `MYSQL_CAPS_CU` in the code and `mysql.caps.cu` in the interface. And this is know as `CLIENT_PROTOCOL_41` on https://dev.mysql.com/doc/dev/mysql-server/latest/group__group__cs__capabilities__flags.html and in `mysql_com.h`.\n\nOne solution might be to go for a combination of 1 and 2 where a version can be chosen or be set to \"Custom\" and then let the user modify individual flags.\n\n---\n\nFor option 3: Besides having \"caps\" and \"caps_ext\" for both client and server we also have `mariadb_server_ext_caps` and `mariadb_client_ext_caps`.\n\nMaybe the interface could look a bit like this:\n\n| Capability Set | Client | Server |\n|---|---|---|\n| Basic | 0xf7fe | 0xf7fe |\n| Extended | 0x81bf | 0x81bf |\n| MariaDB Extended | 0x0 | 0x0 |\n\nAnd then we ideally would have an option to make it easy/obvious for someone to copy these flags from an earlier (full) conversation.",
          "author": {
            "id": 5884947,
            "username": "dveeden",
            "public_email": "",
            "name": "Daniël van Eeden",
            "state": "active",
            "locked": false,
            "avatar_url": "https://secure.gravatar.com/avatar/00c536bb5304097eb409787c4a770e820db2a200d04130b8ca03cbcda0f526f7?s=80&d=identicon",
            "web_url": "https://gitlab.com/dveeden"
          },
          "created_at": "2024-05-27T06:17:41.152Z",
          "updated_at": "2024-05-27T06:17:41.152Z",
          "system": false,
